--TEST--
a malformed literal argument to an intrinsic is refused while checking, and the same text behind a variable is not (`rule:expressions/intrinsic-list-is-closed`, `rule:expressions/intrinsic-literals` and `rule:expressions/preparation-preserves-behaviour`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The other half of `core/a-literal-intrinsic-is-validated-while-checking.nvst`.
// There the two paths agree on an answer; here the literal path reports, at its
// own span, the error the first call would have thrown.
//
// Every row of § 1's table whose grammar has a reader is written twice — once
// with the malformed text as a literal, once with the identical text behind a
// `string` binding. Only the literal spellings are reported, and the count in
// the final line is what asserts it: § 2 refuses nothing for being dynamic, so
// the variable-borne twins add no diagnostic and are left to run time.

var $utc = Core\Time\Zone::UTC;
var $when = Core\Time::at(2026, 8, 28, $utc);

// `Core\Time\DateTime::format`, argument 0 — a CLDR pattern letter no library
// carries.
var $badDate = "yyyy-jjjjjj";
var $foldedDate = $when->format("yyyy-jjjjjj");
var $runtimeDate = $when->format($badDate);

// `Core\Time::parse`, argument *1* — the subject is the text being read, so the
// pattern is not at position 0 (`rule:core-api/shape-rules` R1).
var $badParse = "yyyy-jjjjjj";
var $foldedParsed = Core\Time::parse("2026-08-28", "yyyy-jjjjjj", $utc);
var $runtimeParsed = Core\Time::parse("2026-08-28", $badParse, $utc);

// `Core\Regex::compile`, argument 0 — a group neither engine can close.
var $badPattern = "^order-(\\d+$";
var $foldedPattern = Core\Regex::compile("^order-(\\d+$");
var $runtimePattern = Core\Regex::compile($badPattern);

// `Core\Str::format`, argument 0 — a conversion the template grammar does not
// have. This is the row checked against the *call*, not just the literal.
var $badTemplate = "total: %q";
var $foldedText = Core\Str::format("total: %q", 42);
var $runtimeText = Core\Str::format($badTemplate, 42);

// `Core\Time\Duration::parse`, argument 0 — units that do not descend. The
// parser is `rule:types/duration-literal`'s one implementation, shared with the lexer, so this
// is the same sentence the literal `30m1h` gets.
var $badDuration = "30m1h";
var $foldedDuration = Core\Time\Duration::parse("30m1h");
var $runtimeDuration = Core\Time\Duration::parse($badDuration);

// `Core\Uri::parse`, argument 0 — a byte the URI grammar does not admit.
var $badUri = "https://exa mple.test/x";
var $foldedUri = Core\Uri::parse("https://exa mple.test/x");
var $runtimeUri = Core\Uri::parse($badUri);

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0769]: this literal is not one this member can read: `j` is not a pattern letter this library carries; quote it as `'j'` to emit it literally
%A
error[E0769]: this literal is not one this member can read: `j` is not a pattern letter this library carries; quote it as `'j'` to emit it literally
%A
error[E0769]: this literal is not one this member can read: `^order-(\d+$` is not a pattern either engine can compile:%A
error[E0769]: this literal is not one this member can read: `%%q` is not one of the conversions the template grammar allows (`%%s %%d %%u %%f %%e %%g %%x %%X %%o %%b %%%%`)
%A
error[E0769]: this literal is not one this member can read: duration units strictly descend and may not repeat, so `h` may not follow `m`
%A
error[E0769]: this literal is not one this member can read: this text is not a URI reference (RFC 3986 § 4.1)%A
error: aborting due to 6 errors
